    <h1>Odin: the Raven-God</h1>
    <p class="lore-subtitle">Hrafnaguð — Why Odin Bears the Raven Title</p>
  </header>

  <section class="lore-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p>Odin is called <em>Hrafnaguð</em> ("Raven-God") because his two ravens Huginn and Muninn fly the nine worlds each dawn and return with news. The name appears in Snorri Sturluson's <em>Gylfaginning</em> (c. 1220 CE), chapter 38, as one of Odin's heiti — his recognised by-names or epithets.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Hrafnaguð — the Textual Evidence</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In <em>Gylfaginning</em> ch. 38, Snorri Sturluson writes (paraphrase): "Because of these ravens he is called the Raven-God (<em>Hrafnaguð</em>)." The passage comes immediately after his account of Huginn and Muninn and his quotation of <em>Grímnismál</em> stanza 20.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The by-name <em>Hrafnaguð</em> does not appear in the <em>Poetic Edda</em> itself — it is Snorri's explanatory label. It is, however, consistent with the broader use of <em>heiti</em> (by-names) for Odin across both Eddas and skaldic poetry. Rudolf Simek lists it among Odin's names in his <em>Dictionary of Northern Mythology</em> (1993, p. 160).<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Odin's Ravens as an Expression of His Attributes</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Odin is god of wisdom, war, poetry, and the dead. His ravens bridge these domains:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Wisdom:</strong> Huginn (Thought) and Muninn (Memory) embody Odin's two most valued faculties. Without thought and memory, he loses the omniscience that is his chief power.</li>
      <li><strong>War and death:</strong> Ravens are carrion birds; in Old Norse poetry they are synonymous with the slain and the battlefield. A god of war would naturally be attended by ravens.</li>
      <li><strong>Omniscience:</strong> The daily flight across the nine worlds mirrors Odin's role as the all-seeing god who sits on Hliðskjálf (his high-seat) and surveys the cosmos.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> This triple connection — cognition, death, omniscience — is what distinguishes Odin's ravens from mere pets or symbols. They are, as Hilda Ellis Davidson argued, extensions of Odin's own mind projected outward.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>The Beasts of Battle: Ravens at the Viking Battlefield</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Old Norse poetry uses a convention called "the beasts of battle": before or after a fight, ravens, wolves (<em>Geri</em> and <em>Freki</em>, also Odin's companions), and eagles anticipate the feast of the fallen. This is not unique to Norse literature — the same motif appears in Old English and Old High German poetry — but in Norse sources it is especially tightly bound to Odin.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens do follow large mammals including human groups, and historically gathered at battlefields, executions, and refuse sites — behaviours documented by ornithologists.<sup><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup> The literary convention has a basis in observable raven behaviour.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Other Raven By-Names of Odin</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Beyond <em>Hrafnaguð</em>, Odin's connection to ravens is reflected in several other heiti noted in scholarly editions of the Eddas and skaldic poetry:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Hrafnáss</em> — "Raven-God/Raven-Áss", attested in skaldic kennings.</li>
      <li><em>Hrafn</em> — simply "Raven", used as a kenning base for Odin in some verses.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These names are catalogued in Simek (1993) and in Snorri's <em>Skáldskaparmál</em>, the section of the Prose Edda that lists poetic names and kennings.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section lore-bridge">
    <h2>The Birds Behind the Story</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>) has been the dominant large corvid of Scandinavia throughout the Viking Age and before. Its intelligence, its habit of following human activity, and its jet-black plumage made it the natural choice as emblem for a god of wisdom and death.<sup><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup></p>
